        <content type="html">&lt;p&gt;In this talk with &lt;a href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.linkedin.com&#x2F;in&#x2F;jeremie-monsinjon&#x2F;&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Jeremie Monsinjon&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; we explain what Docker swarm is and how you can use it to orchestrate your containers in a production ready mode.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;youtube is-flex is-justify-content-center is-align-items-center&quot;&gt;
    &lt;iframe
        width=&quot;848&quot; height=&quot;510&quot;
        src=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.youtube.com&#x2F;embed&#x2F;licg4PmK_pk&quot;
        frameborder=&quot;0&quot; 
        allow=&quot;accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture&quot; 
        allowfullscreen&gt;
    &lt;&#x2F;iframe&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;div&gt;&lt;br &#x2F;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The code repository is available in &lt;a href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ImFlog&#x2F;docker-discovery&quot;&gt;GitHub&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Nowadays, we don&#x27;t see anymore Docker Swarm in the wild. Kubernetes has taken over the container orchestration world. But it&#x27;s still interesting to see how it was working and how it was different from Kubernetes.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
